:name: CAN PoC step 4: redundant buses plus a bridge from bus A to the PC
:description: redundant.resc plus Renode's SocketCAN bridge on busA, so Linux tools (candump, cansend, Wireshark) see the simulated bus as vcan0.
:
: Linux only. needs a virtual CAN interface on the PC first (once per boot, needs sudo):
:   sudo modprobe vcan
:   sudo ip link add dev vcan0 type vcan
:   sudo ip link set up vcan0
:   sudo apt install can-utils (optional, for candump / cansend)
:
: how to run (from the top folder, the one with firmware/ and renode/):
:   renode renode/socketcan_bridge.resc then type: start
:   candump -td vcan0 in another terminal
:   cansend vcan0 100#0300DEADBEEF send a frame in --> the experiment prints it
:
:   obc.can1 ----+---- busA ----+---- experiment.can1
:                |              |
:                +-- vcanA -----+--> Linux vcan0
:   obc.can2 --------- busB --------- experiment.can2
:
: the bridge vcanA is just one more thing plugged into busA, it passes frames
: between the hub and the Linux interface both ways
: to see bus B too, make vcan1 the same way and uncomment the last 3 lines

$art=@renode/art/socketcan_bridge.py
include @renode/redundant.resc

# in Renode the bridge has to belong to a machine, which one doesn't matter
mach set "obc"
machine CreateSocketCANBridge "vcanA" "vcan0"
connector Connect vcanA busA

# machine CreateSocketCANBridge "vcanB" "vcan1"
# connector Connect vcanB busB
mach clear
